<TopicPage slug="subjects/history/intermediate/revolutions-of-1848">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cambridge’s Option A core opens with exactly this lesson’s question: <strong>Were the
    revolutions of 1848 important?</strong> In eighteen months, almost every capital between
    Paris and Vienna erupted — the “springtime of peoples”. As a series of seizures of power,
    1848 was a catastrophe: nearly every revolution was defeated within two years. As a
    turning point, it is hard to overstate: <strong>democracy, nationalism and the method of
    unifying states from above all date their modern careers to this failure</strong>. The
    syllabus asks you to hold both truths — causes, events and results in France, Italy,
    Germany and the Austrian Empire, and an honest verdict on what failed and what didn’t.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the revolution, month by month — use the chips to compare the four theatres" widget="timeline" config={timelineCfg} />

  <p>
    Drag the scrubber slowly. What jumps out is <em>simultaneity</em>: within six weeks of
    February, barricades were up in four empires. That is no accident — the same bad harvests,
    the same ideas, and news travelling by railway and telegraph made 1848 a single European
    event wearing local costumes.
  </p>

  <h2>Why 1848 — and why everywhere</h2>
  <p>
    Two long-term forces were ready and waiting. <strong>Liberalism</strong>, the creed of the
    growing middle classes, demanded constitutions, free press, jury courts and an end to
    censorship and privileged bureaucracy — the Congress system that Metternich had run since
    1815 was designed to prevent exactly this. <strong>Nationalism</strong> demanded that
    peoples, not dynasties, draw the map: Italians under Austrian and local princely rule,
    Germans split into 39 states, Czechs, Magyars, Poles and South Slavs governed by a German-speaking
    Habsburg court. The two creeds were allies while they fought the old order — and rivals
    the moment they had to share the future.
  </p>
  <p>
    The trigger was economic. Blighted potatoes and failed grain harvests in 1845–47, then a
    financial crash in 1847, meant hungry crowds in the cities just as liberal grievances
    peaked. When Paris’s February revolution produced a republic in seventy-two hours, every
    government in Europe faced the same arithmetic: concede fast or fall fast. Most conceded —
    constitutions, press freedom, national assemblies — before the counter-attack came.
    By December 1849, virtually every concession that mattered had been taken back.
  </p>

  <h2>Why most of them failed — and what changed anyway</h2>
  <p>
    The revolutions shared a fatal internal split: the middle class wanted political rights,
    the urban poor wanted bread and jobs, and the June Days in Paris — workers shot in the
    streets by the republic’s own army — became the symbol of the divorce. In the multi-national
    empires, nationalities cancelled each other out. In the end the armed forces stayed loyal
    (nowhere more decisively than in Habsburg Hungary, saved by a Russian army), and the
    Frankfurt Parliament showed that a national assembly without a national army is a debating
    society. Walk through the whole arc, then the afterlife:
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the arc of 1848 — rise, pattern, failure, and the changes that stuck" widget="equationStep" config={arcCfg} />

  <h2>So — were they important?</h2>
  <p>
    Judge it as the focus points do, weighing short-term failure against long-term consequence.
    <strong>France</strong> kept the revolution’s biggest prize, universal male suffrage, which
    then produced Napoleon III — a lesson in how democracy and dictatorship can share a ballot
    box. <strong>Austria</strong> abolished serfdom in September 1848 and never restored it; a
    conservative state learned to rule a free peasantry, and peasants never rebelled for the
    liberals again. <strong>Germany and Italy</strong> learned the century’s most consequential
    lesson: idealism without armies fails, so unification would be engineered from above —
    Cavour’s diplomacy and Bismarck’s wars are both answers to 1848. The revolutionaries’
    defeats, in other words, wrote the script for the next twenty years. That is what “Were
    the revolutions of 1848 important?” is really asking you to argue.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Cambridge IGCSE History 0470 syllabus (2027–2028), Core Option A key question 1; Hobsbawm, The Age of Revolution; Rapport, 1848: Year of Revolution.</p>
